WEBVTT

00:00.400 --> 00:03.840
Which parameters of your model fit your data best?

00:04.090 --> 00:07.620
The ones that minimize the error, or make your data most probable.

00:07.970 --> 00:09.780
This is an optimization problem.

00:10.040 --> 00:16.760
Picture the error as a landscape: the lower the ground, the better the fit. You want the lowest point.

00:17.060 --> 00:23.260
But you can't see the landscape. For many computational models, the error is approximated numerically, or noisy,

00:23.260 --> 00:28.460
so its slope is no guide, and each evaluation can take seconds: they are black boxes.

00:29.160 --> 00:34.650
Bayesian optimization fits a surrogate to the points it has seen: a statistical model of the whole landscape.

00:34.900 --> 00:40.080
It evaluates where the ground looks low, or might be. Then it updates the surrogate, and repeats.

00:40.680 --> 00:43.590
When the surrogate is accurate, this is smart and fast.

00:44.190 --> 00:50.130
But the surrogate can be wrong. If the landscape has a shape it can't capture, it keeps pointing to the wrong places.

00:50.830 --> 00:53.670
Instead, direct search uses no surrogate.

00:53.990 --> 00:59.480
From the best point so far, it tries a step in each direction, on a grid called the mesh.

00:59.860 --> 01:06.590
If a step finds lower ground, it moves there and takes bigger steps. If none does, it takes smaller ones.

01:07.100 --> 01:11.700
It can't be fooled, and it comes with convergence guarantees. But it is slow.

01:12.400 --> 01:15.860
Bayesian Adaptive Direct Search, or BADS, combines the two.

01:16.180 --> 01:23.100
It alternates a SEARCH stage, Bayesian optimization around the best point, and a POLL stage, a round of direct search.*

01:23.480 --> 01:30.860
As long as the SEARCH finds better points, BADS keeps searching. When it fails several times in a row, BADS polls.

01:31.240 --> 01:33.140
The two stages help each other.

01:33.400 --> 01:41.740
When the surrogate fails, the POLL moves away from the region it can't model, and the points it collects help build a better surrogate for the next SEARCH.

01:42.440 --> 01:45.660
Here is a real PyBADS run, on the same landscape.

01:45.980 --> 01:51.230
It starts with a few evaluations and fits the surrogate. The SEARCH heads downhill fast.

01:52.000 --> 01:59.810
But the trench is too narrow for the surrogate. From one deep point, it predicts deep ground where there is none, and four searches in a row fail.

02:00.580 --> 02:08.040
So PyBADS polls. One step lands in the trench, far lower than any point before, and the mesh grows.

02:08.930 --> 02:12.490
The points from the POLL show the surrogate the shape of the trench.

02:13.000 --> 02:17.720
Now the SEARCH is fast again: its next point lands near the bottom of the trench.

02:18.360 --> 02:23.120
When no step improves any more, the mesh shrinks, and PyBADS settles on the minimum.

02:23.820 --> 02:27.140
PyBADS never saw the true landscape. Here it is.

02:27.780 --> 02:32.240
Smart and fast when the surrogate is right. Slow but steady when it's wrong.

02:32.940 --> 02:40.000
That landscape had two parameters. BADS works with up to twenty, on rugged or noisy landscapes.

02:40.440 --> 02:46.720
On dozens of real model-fitting problems, it matched or beat sixteen other optimizers.*

02:49.220 --> 02:55.980
Since then, it has been used in hundreds of studies, from brains and quantum computers to oil wells and wildfires.*

02:56.620 --> 02:57.570
Join them.
